In a microscopic picture, light (anti)nucleus can be formed by coalescence during the last stage of the relativistic heavy-ion collisions. The production rate for light (anti)nucleus is proportional to the nucleon density in the phase space, therefore, presents an exponential behavior with respect to the baryon number. On the other hand, the statistic model, which regards the collision system as a fireball at a extremely high temperature, light (anti)nucleus, regarded as an object with energy \(E=|\mathrm{B}|m_{p}\), can be emitted by the system with a probability determined by the Boltzmann factor \(e^{-E/T}\). This model also produces the yield as an exponential of the baryon number. The considerations outlined above offer a good estimation for the production rate of heavier antinuclei. The yield of the stable antimatter nucleus next in line (\({}^{6}\overline{\mathrm{Li}}\)) is predicted to be down by a factor of \(2.6\times 10^{6}\) compared to \({}^{4}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) and is beyond the reach of current accelerator technology.

### 4.5 Backgrounds and corrections

#### Background estimation

Due to the finite TOF timing resolution and the mis-matching between TPC tracks and TOF hits, the \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) mass distribution may be extended to the \({}^{4}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) selection window, and contributes to the background of \({}^{4}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\).

To determine the background contribution due to the finite TOF timing resolution, the \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) mass distribution is reproduced with each \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) track's expected time of flight, but smeared by time deviation (\(t-t^{expected}\)) of \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) from the same data sample. Similarly, to estimate the background contribution from the mis-matching between TPC tracks and corresponding TOF hits, the \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) mass value is calculated using each \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) track's expected time of flight and time deviation from a random data sample. Figure 4-13 presents the reproduced n\(\sigma^{{}^{4}He}_{dE/dx}\) versus mass distribution. By projecting to the mass axis with a \(n\sigma^{{}^{4}He}_{dE/dx}\) cut from -2.0 to 3.0. (Figure 4-14), the contamination of \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) to \({}^{4}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) can be estimated by integrating the reproduced \({}^{3}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) mass distribution over the mass selection window of 3.35 GeV/c\({}^{2}\)\(<\) mass \(<\) 4.04 GeV/c\({}^{2}\). The background contributes 1.4 and 0.05 counts of the 15 and 1 counts from the Au+Au collisions at 200 and 62GeV, respectively. The probability of misidentification, calculated in the way of Poisson distribution, is \(10^{-11}\), corresponding to a signal significance larger than \(6\sigma\). The background of \({}^{4}\overline{\mathrm{He}}\) and \({}^{4}\mathrm{He}\) with different \(n\sigma\) cuts are also calculated and presented in table 4-4.

