### 5.3 Particle ratios

The \(p_{T}\) integrated yields (dN/dy) for light (anti)nuclei, and (anti)hypertriton were calculated at middle rapidity, as presented in table 5.2. Furthermore, the relative particle production abundances of light (anti)nuclei and (anti)hypernuclei are investigated and compared with RHIC data and the thermal model predictions.

Figure 5.4 shows the particle ratios for light (anti)nuclei and (anti)hypernuclei. Our results can fit the ratios of antinuclei to nuclei as well as \({}^{4}\)He/\({}^{3}\)He and \({}^{4}\overline{\rm He}\)/\({}^{3}\overline{\rm He}\) at RHIC energy very well [9, 31, 56, 75], and are also consistent with thermal model predictions. However, for \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H/\({}^{3}\)He and \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\overline{\rm H}\)/\({}^{3}\overline{\rm He}\), the coalescence production mechanism has a better description than thermal results as declaredin reference[32].

### 5.4 Coalescence parameter \(B_{a}\)

The coalescence parameters \(B_{A}\) can be evaluated by comparing the invariant yields of the light (anti)nuclei and the primordial (anti)nucleons. \(B_{A}\) is interpreted to be inversely related to the fireball volume in coordinate space, i.e., \(B_{A}\propto 1/V^{A-1}\)[16], which was confirmed by the energy dependence of \(B_{A}\)[48]. Figure 5-5 presents \(\sqrt[A]{B_{A}}\) as a function of transverse momentum. \(B_{2}\), \(\sqrt{B_{3}}\), and \(\sqrt[3]{B_{4}}\) calculated based on the invariant yields of \(\rm d(\overline{\rm d})\), \({}^{3}He(^{3}\overline{He})\), \(\sqrt[3]{{}^{3}_{\Lambda}H}(^{3}_{\Lambda}\overline{H})\) and \({}^{4}He(^{4}\overline{He})\) are consistent with the STAR \(B_{2}\) results in central collisions[48]. The PHENIX measurements with \(\rm d(\overline{\rm d})\) spectra in other centralities are also included in the figure for reference[56]. Combining RHIC data and our calculation, the coalescence parameters \(B_{A}\) show a strong centrality dependence as shown in Figure 5-5, which means central collision usually create a larger fireball volume than peripheral one[48, 56]. On the other hand, the \(p_{T}\) dependence of \(\sqrt[A]{B_{A}}\) is related to the position-momentum correlations in an expanding fireball, and provides information for the nucleon density profile and radial flow[56]. In addition, it is noticed that \(\sqrt{B_{3}}\) of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}H\) is smaller than that of \({}^{3}He\) even though the two nuclei have the same mass number. This reflects the strangeness content dependence of the coalescence parameter, i.e, there exists an additional penalty factor due to strangeness. Overall, this calculation with an uniform nucleon density plus an outward velocity can reproduce the PHENIX results, which also consistent with previous model calculations[16,

